<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  generalize_answer.xml.j2
  Custom Sysprep answer file for OSOT's Generalize pass (roles/osot_generalize).
  Templated by Ansible's win_template - the double-curly-brace syntax below
  is Jinja2, NOT the
  $${...} Terraform interpolation floppy/autounattend.pkrtpl.hcl uses for the
  FIRST boot's answer file. Deliberately much smaller than that file: this
  only needs to get Sysprep's OOBE mini-setup back to a state where WinRM is
  reachable again, not build the machine's identity from scratch - no
  DiskConfiguration/ImageInstall/UserData/ComputerName, those are
  first-install-only (and this image is never re-specialized with a new
  computer name here - Horizon Instant Clone renames/joins each spawned
  host individually at clone time, same as it always has for this image;
  see rdsh_2025_tpl.pkr.hcl's own header on that).

  Architecture: reseals to full OOBE with AutoLogon - the user's explicit
  choice, given via this project's AskUserQuestion tool, over an Audit-mode
  reseal (Mode=Audit) - since this pipeline has no MDT-LiteTouch-style
  continuation agent to pick a task sequence back up automatically either
  way, and Audit mode would need one. AutoLogon signs the build
  Administrator account back in once (LogonCount=1), FirstLogonCommands
  below then re-enables WinRM - a trimmed copy of autounattend.pkrtpl.hcl's
  own oobeSystem/FirstLogonCommands block. BitLocker-prevention and the
  VMware Tools install are NOT repeated here: both already landed
  permanently on first boot, and Sysprep's generalize doesn't undo either.

  CopyProfile (specialize pass - Microsoft's own documented requirement,
  NOT valid in oobeSystem - confirmed against Microsoft's own
  Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup|CopyProfile schema reference, which states
  outright "CopyProfile runs during the specialize configuration pass") -
  copies the build Administrator profile - by this point in the pipeline
  holding every optimization/app install this project has done - into the
  Default User profile, so every future Instant Clone session inherits it
  instead of starting from a bare default profile.

  MERGED 2026-09-15 against OSOT's own GUI-generated Generalize answer file
  (View Answer File, with the GUI's own Time Zone/Locale/Autologon/Copy
  Profile fields filled in) - the user pasted its full real output so this
  could be compared and merged accurately rather than guessed. What got
  pulled in vs. deliberately left out, and why:

    PULLED IN:
    - pass="generalize" SkipRearm=1 (Microsoft-Windows-Security-SPP) - new.
      Tells Sysprep to skip decrementing the rearm counter, directly
      addressing the "Generalize consumes one of Sysprep's limited rearm
      counts" cost this role's own header and enter_audit_mode's header
      both flag as a real, non-trivial cost of iterating on this pipeline.
    - pass="specialize" RunSynchronous (Microsoft-Windows-Deployment):
      `net accounts /maxpwage:unlimited` + `net user Administrator
      /active:yes` - defense-in-depth alongside the password-expiry fixes
      this project already fought hard to get right (see
      floppy/autounattend.pkrtpl.hcl's own auditUser RunSynchronous block for
      that saga - it now carries the WinRM-bootstrap sequence that used to
      live in roles/enter_audit_mode/templates/audit_answer.xml.j2, since
      deleted) and a belt-and-suspenders guarantee the built-in Administrator
      stays enabled through the reseal.
    - pass="specialize" TimeZone (Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup) - new,
      wasn't set anywhere in this file before. Uses the SAME `timezone`
      Ansible variable (inventory/group_vars/rdsh_2025_tpl.yml) already
      used for the FIRST boot's answer file, which happens to already equal
      OSOT's own GUI-generated value ("W. Europe Standard Time") exactly -
      good independent confirmation that variable is already correct.
    - FirstLogonCommands housekeeping block (RetailDemo cleanup, Default
      User profile registry tweaks, IE/zone security relaxation,
      LowRiskFileTypes, disabling the WindowsUpdate scheduled task,
      deleting the Sysprep answer file) - genuinely useful, standard
      golden-image hygiene this file didn't do before. Appended AFTER this
      file's own pre-existing WinRM-bootstrap commands (which stay first,
      unchanged, Orders 1-9) as Orders 10-20, renumbered to preserve OSOT's
      own REAL intended execution order (by Order value, not document
      position) - notably its own generated file lists "delete
      unattend.xml" at Order 20 (deliberately last, well after
      "disable WindowsUpdate" at Order 11) despite writing it earlier in
      the XML document; that relative sequence is preserved here, not the
      document order. All given RequiresUserInput=false explicitly (OSOT's
      own file left its very first entry at =true, most likely a generator
      artifact rather than intentional) to match every other command in
      this file's own already-proven-safe convention.

    NOT PULLED IN, deliberately:
    - PlainText=false + Base64 Password/AdministratorPassword values.
      Decoding OSOT's own "UABhAHMAcwB3AG8AcgBkAA==" gives literal UTF-16LE
      for the word "Password" - clearly a GUI placeholder the user typed to
      generate a preview, not a real credential. This file already uses
      PlainText=true with {{ vault_win_admin_password }} directly below,
      which is simpler and is the exact mechanism this project spent most
      of a session getting right (see floppy/autounattend.pkrtpl.hcl's own
      auditSystem AdministratorPassword/AutoLogon pairing - the content that
      used to live in roles/enter_audit_mode/templates/audit_answer.xml.j2,
      now deleted, moved there verbatim) - not touching that.
    - The new UserAccounts/LocalAccounts/LocalAccount wcm:action="add"
      block (targeting "Administrator", adding it to "Administrators;Remote
      Desktop Users"). wcm:action="add" is documented for CREATING a new
      account; Administrator already exists as a built-in account, and
      nobody has confirmed how Windows Setup handles "add" against an
      existing built-in one - not worth guessing that into a Sysprep pass
      where failures are painful to debug. If Administrator needs to be in
      Remote Desktop Users specifically, a `net localgroup` FirstLogonCommand
      is the lower-risk way to get there - not added here since it wasn't
      asked for, easy to add on request.
    - OSOT's own placement of CopyProfile in the oobeSystem pass. Per
      Microsoft's own schema reference (see this header's earlier
      paragraph), CopyProfile is only valid in the specialize pass - OSOT's
      GUI-generated file has this in the wrong pass, silently a no-op there
      rather than an error. Kept in specialize, where it already was and
      where it actually works.

  ADDED 2026-09-18: `net accounts /lockoutthreshold:0` in the specialize
  RunSynchronous block below - this build failed with "ntlm: the specified
  credentials were rejected by the server" on the very first task after
  reconnecting post-Sysprep (store_apps_cleanup), right after
  wait_for_connection had already succeeded once and
  osot_generalize/tasks/main.yml's own reset_connection had run cleanly -
  the same symptom this project already root-caused once before, on this
  same account, during the FIRST boot's audit-mode entry (see
  floppy/autounattend.pkrtpl.hcl's own Order 4 comment in its specialize
  pass for the full investigation: net use over SMB proved the account was
  genuinely locked out, not misconfigured, and WinRM/WS-Man itself never
  surfaces lockout distinctly from a wrong password - both report the same
  generic "credentials rejected"). That file's specialize pass disables
  lockout entirely (`net accounts /lockoutthreshold:0`) specifically so it
  protects every WinRM reconnection for the rest of the build, but this
  file's own specialize RunSynchronous block never carried the same command
  - Sysprep's specialize pass here re-specializes the machine independently
  of whatever the first boot's answer file already set, so nothing here
  reapplied it. Added as Order 3, before AutoLogon/WinRM bootstrap in the
  oobeSystem pass below ever run, same placement reasoning as the original.
-->
<unattend xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:unattend">

  <settings pass="generalize">
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Security-SPP"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State"
               xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <SkipRearm>1</SkipRearm>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <settings pass="specialize">

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Deployment"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State"
               xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <RunSynchronous>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Path>net accounts /maxpwage:unlimited</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>2</Order>
          <Path>net user Administrator /active:yes</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <!-- See this file's own header, "ADDED 2026-09-18" - same fix
             floppy/autounattend.pkrtpl.hcl's specialize pass already proved
             for this exact account/symptom on first boot, reapplied here
             since Sysprep's own specialize pass doesn't carry it forward on
             its own. -->
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>3</Order>
          <Path>net accounts /lockoutthreshold:0</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
      </RunSynchronous>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State"
               xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <CopyProfile>true</CopyProfile>
      <TimeZone>{{ timezone }}</TimeZone>
    </component>

  </settings>

  <settings pass="oobeSystem">

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State"
               xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <InputLocale>{{ win_keyboard }}</InputLocale>
      <SystemLocale>{{ win_language }}</SystemLocale>
      <UILanguage>{{ win_language }}</UILanguage>
      <UserLocale>{{ win_language }}</UserLocale>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State"
               xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">

      <OOBE>
        <HideEULAPage>true</HideEULAPage>
        <HideLocalAccountScreen>true</HideLocalAccountScreen>
        <HideOEMRegistrationScreen>true</HideOEMRegistrationScreen>
        <HideOnlineAccountScreens>true</HideOnlineAccountScreens>
        <HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>true</HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>
        <NetworkLocation>Work</NetworkLocation>
        <ProtectYourPC>3</ProtectYourPC>
        <SkipMachineOOBE>true</SkipMachineOOBE>
        <SkipUserOOBE>true</SkipUserOOBE>
      </OOBE>

      <UserAccounts>
        <AdministratorPassword>
          <Value>{{ vault_win_admin_password }}</Value>
          <PlainText>true</PlainText>
        </AdministratorPassword>
      </UserAccounts>

      <AutoLogon>
        <Password>
          <Value>{{ vault_win_admin_password }}</Value>
          <PlainText>true</PlainText>
        </Password>
        <Enabled>true</Enabled>
        <LogonCount>1</LogonCount>
        <Username>Administrator</Username>
      </AutoLogon>

      <FirstLogonCommands>

        <!-- â”€â”€ WinRM bootstrap only - trimmed from autounattend.pkrtpl.hcl's
             oobeSystem pass (see this file's header comment for why
             BitLocker-prevention and VMware Tools aren't repeated here).
             Network location resetting to Public after Sysprep - which
             re-blocks WinRM behind Windows Firewall's Public profile - is
             the well-known gotcha this specifically guards against. â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€ -->
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <!-- ADDED 2026-09-14, same "must change password at next logon"
               clear as autounattend.pkrtpl.hcl's own oobeSystem pass (see
               that file's comment for the full evidence/reasoning) - this
               file also sets <AdministratorPassword> above via the unattend
               UserAccounts element, the same mechanism implicated there, so
               defending here too rather than assuming a Sysprep reseal
               can't reintroduce the same flag. -->
          <Description>Clear "must change password at next logon" on Administrator, and set password to never expire (see autounattend.pkrtpl.hcl's specialize-pass comment on why the plain clear alone isn't durable across a Sysprep transition)</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd.exe /c powershell -Command "Set-LocalUser -Name Administrator -PasswordNeverExpires $true; $u=[ADSI]('WinNT://'+$env:COMPUTERNAME+'/Administrator,user'); $u.Put('PasswordExpired',0); $u.SetInfo()"</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>2</Order>
          <Description>Enable WinRM</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd.exe /c winrm quickconfig -quiet</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>3</Order>
          <Description>Set WinRM service to automatic startup</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd.exe /c sc config winrm start= auto</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>4</Order>
          <Description>Allow unencrypted WinRM (build network only)</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd.exe /c winrm set winrm/config/service @{AllowUnencrypted="true"}</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>5</Order>
          <Description>Enable WinRM Basic auth</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd.exe /c winrm set winrm/config/service/auth @{Basic="true"}</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>6</Order>
          <!-- ADDED 2026-09-14, same NTLM-transport trial as
               floppy/autounattend.pkrtpl.hcl's own auditUser
               RunSynchronous block (which now carries the WinRM-bootstrap
               sequence that used to live in
               roles/enter_audit_mode/templates/audit_answer.xml.j2, since
               deleted) - see that file's own comments for the full
               reasoning. Kept identical across both WinRM-bootstrap points
               on purpose, so the service's auth config never differs
               depending on which reboot last ran this. -->
          <Description>Enable WinRM Negotiate (NTLM) auth</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd.exe /c winrm set winrm/config/service/auth @{Negotiate="true"}</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>7</Order>
          <Description>Open WinRM firewall port</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd.exe /c netsh advfirewall firewall add rule name="WinRM-HTTP" dir=in action=allow protocol=TCP localport=5985</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>8</Order>
          <Description>Set network profile to Private</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd.exe /c powershell -Command "Get-NetConnectionProfile | Set-NetConnectionProfile -NetworkCategory Private"</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>9</Order>
          <Description>Restart WinRM to apply config</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd.exe /c net stop winrm &amp; net start winrm</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>

        <!-- â”€â”€ OSOT-sourced golden-image housekeeping, appended 2026-09-15 -
             see this file's own header for where these came from and why
             they're renumbered 10-20 in OSOT's own real intended execution
             order rather than its generated document order. â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€ -->
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>10</Order>
          <Description>Disable RetailDemo CleanupOfflineContent scheduled task (OSOT-sourced)</Description>
          <CommandLine>schtasks /change /TN "\Microsoft\Windows\RetailDemo\CleanupOfflineContent" /Disable</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>11</Order>
          <Description>Load the Default User registry hive (OSOT-sourced)</Description>
          <CommandLine>reg load "HKEY_USERS\temp" "%USERPROFILE%\..\Default User\NTUSER.DAT"</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>12</Order>
          <Description>Opt the Default User profile out of HTTP Accept-Language header collection (OSOT-sourced)</Description>
          <CommandLine>reg ADD "HKEY_USERS\temp\Control Panel\International\User Profile" /v HttpAcceptLanguageOptout /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000001 /f</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>13</Order>
          <Description>Unload the Default User registry hive (OSOT-sourced)</Description>
          <CommandLine>reg unload "HKEY_USERS\temp"</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>14</Order>
          <Description>Disable Reserved Storage on this generalized image (OSOT-sourced)</Description>
          <CommandLine>reg ADD "HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\ReserveManager" /v ShippedWithReserves /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000000 /f</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>15</Order>
          <Description>Disable Internet Explorer Enhanced Security Configuration's zone checks (OSOT-sourced)</Description>
          <CommandLine>reg ADD "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Internet Explorer\Security" /v "DisableSecuritySettingsCheck" /t "REG_DWORD" /D "00000001" /f</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>16</Order>
          <Description>Relax IE zone 3 policy 1806 for HKCU (OSOT-sourced)</Description>
          <CommandLine>reg ADD "HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Internet Settings\Zones\3" /v "1806" /t "REG_DWORD" /D "00000000" /f</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>17</Order>
          <Description>Relax IE zone 3 policy 1806 for HKLM (OSOT-sourced)</Description>
          <CommandLine>reg ADD "HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Internet Settings\Zones\3" /v "1806" /t "REG_DWORD" /D "00000000" /f</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>18</Order>
          <Description>Mark .exe as a low-risk file type to suppress the Open File security prompt (OSOT-sourced)</Description>
          <CommandLine>reg ADD "HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\Associations" /v "LowRiskFileTypes" /t "REG_SZ" /D ".exe" /f</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>19</Order>
          <Description>Disable the Windows Update "Scheduled Start" scheduled task (OSOT-sourced)</Description>
          <CommandLine>schtasks /change /TN "\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate\Scheduled Start" /Disable</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>20</Order>
          <!-- Kept last on purpose, matching OSOT's own real intended order
               (its generated file numbers this 20, well after even Order 11
               above, despite writing it earlier in the document - Order
               VALUE controls execution sequence, not document position).
               This targets the canonical C:\Windows\System32\Sysprep\
               unattend.xml path OSOT's own default workflow uses - this
               pipeline passes its OWN answer file from C:\Temp\ via an
               explicit -g argument instead, so this may be a harmless no-op
               here (nothing at that path to delete); kept anyway as cheap,
               standard security hygiene (removing any leftover Sysprep
               answer file) matching OSOT's own convention. -->
          <Description>Delete any leftover Sysprep answer file at its canonical location (OSOT-sourced, security hygiene)</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd /C del c:\Windows\System32\Sysprep\unattend.xml /Q</CommandLine>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
        </SynchronousCommand>

      </FirstLogonCommands>

    </component>
  </settings>

</unattend>
